# Rank 1656 / CVE-2022-4724: rdiffweb stored each user's SSH public keys in a
# `sshkeys` table keyed by the key's text, which it declared `unique=True`, but
# the `fingerprint` column -- the MD5 of the key material, and the value SSH
# tooling and rdiffweb's own key removal resolve a key by -- carried no
# constraint at all. `UserObject.add_authorizedkey` inserts
# `SshKey(userid=self.userid, fingerprint=key.fingerprint, key=key.getvalue())`
# and catches `IntegrityError` to reject a duplicate key "or one
# associated to another user", so the code claims the boundary while the schema
# only enforces it on the key text: the same fingerprint lands on two accounts,
# and one credential then reaches another user's repositories. The fixed revision declares
# `Index('sshkey_fingerprint_index', SshKey.fingerprint, unique=True)` on the
# model and a startup migration that refuses to upgrade a database still
# holding duplicates; the insert code itself only splits one statement into two.
#
# Concepts considered for reuse: `code.CaseInsensitiveLocalIdentityAuthorizationBypass`
# is the case-folding sibling and does not fit a schema-constraint weakness;
# `code.UnverifiedEmailClaimIdentity` is an OIDC claim-attestation weakness;
# `code.UserIdentityMappingReview` fits unchanged -- "external identity data
# determines local user or group identifiers without an observed uniqueness
# boundary", with the fingerprint as the external identity and `userid` as the
# local identifier -- and this widens it to a third binding and to Python.
#
# The binding reads the model module's assignment tokens: a declarative model
# (`__tablename__`) declaring `fingerprint = Column(...)` beside an owner
# column, with no declaration tying a uniqueness constraint to the
# fingerprint. Assignment tokens are AST-derived, so the commented-out
# constraint test pins that prose cannot silence it.
#
# Measured: the real tree reports one VYQL-AUTH-083 on
# rdiffweb/core/model/_sshkey.py at the vulnerable revision and none at the fixed revision,
# stable over three `--cache off` runs. The shape's reachable set across the
# corpus clones is one other project, an OpenStack keypair table storing a
# fingerprint per user with uniqueness only on (user_id, name): its
# api_models module is reported, while the same table in its thousand-line
# legacy models module falls past the module context's 512-token cap and is
# not -- the recall boundary of reading the declaration at module scope. The
# benchmark corpora contain no `fingerprint` column declaration at all.

test "rank1656: an ssh key model maps fingerprints to accounts with no uniqueness boundary"
  lang python
  file _sshkey.py
  expect VYQL-AUTH-083
  expect_review code.UserIdentityMappingReview
  code
  ```
  from sqlalchemy import Column, Integer, Text
  from sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base

  Base = declarative_base()


  class SshKey(Base):
      __tablename__ = 'sshkeys'
      __table_args__ = {'sqlite_autoincrement': True}
      fingerprint = Column('Fingerprint', Text)
      key = Column('Key', Text, unique=True, primary_key=True)
      userid = Column('UserID', Integer, nullable=False)
  ```

test "rank1656: the fixed revision declares a unique index over the fingerprint"
  lang python
  file _sshkey.py
  reject VYQL-AUTH-083
  code
  ```
  from sqlalchemy import Column, Index, Integer, Text
  from sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base

  Base = declarative_base()


  class SshKey(Base):
      __tablename__ = 'sshkeys'
      __table_args__ = {'sqlite_autoincrement': True}
      fingerprint = Column('Fingerprint', Text)
      key = Column('Key', Text, unique=True, primary_key=True)
      userid = Column('UserID', Integer, nullable=False)


  # Make finger print unique
  sshkey_fingerprint_index = Index('sshkey_fingerprint_index', SshKey.fingerprint, unique=True)
  ```

test "rank1656: the uniqueness flag inline on the column also declares the boundary"
